<section class="page-head"><div class="container"><span class="status-pill completed">Completed</span><div class="eyebrow">Physics · NLP · Time Series · ML</div><h1>Predictive Maintenance for the ATLAS Tile Calorimeter</h1><p class="section-lead">Technical work based on the MSc dissertation “Alert Signal Classification and Prediction Using Natural Language Processing for the Tile Calorimeter of ATLAS.”</p></div></section>
<section><div class="container content">
<section class="story-section"><h2>Project at a glance</h2><div class="story-grid"><div class="story-step"><strong>1 · Objective</strong><p>Assess whether historical ATLAS Tile Calorimeter alarm logs can support earlier identification of equipment problems.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>2 · Data</strong><p>Archived Detector Control System alarm records containing semi-structured information about detector partitions, hardware and modules.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>3 · Method</strong><p>Convert alarm text into structured detector-aware data, then use LSTM models for alarm-count forecasting and dominant alarm-type classification.</p></div><div class="story-step"><strong>4 · Evaluation</strong><p>Preserve chronological order, evaluate forecasting error and multiclass performance, and explicitly account for class imbalance and overlap near one chronological split.</p></div><div class="story-step story-impact"><strong>5 · Outcome</strong><p>The study produced 12.8–22.9% forecasting MAPE and 75% refined classification accuracy, while remaining a research proof-of-concept rather than a deployed maintenance system.</p></div></div></section>
<h2>Context and objective</h2>
<p>The study asks whether archived ATLAS Tile Calorimeter Detector Control System alarm logs can provide a predictive-maintenance layer in addition to their existing monitoring and protection role.</p>

<h2>Data and constraints</h2>
<p>The analysis uses historical Error-level DCS alarm records. The underlying ATLAS/TileCal alarm data are not released publicly. Limited sample sizes, class imbalance and overlap between some sliding windows constrain the strength of operational conclusions.</p>

<h2>Technical solution</h2>
<ul>
<li>Select Error-level DCS alarms and normalise the semi-structured alarm text.</li>
<li>Use targeted NLP and rule-based extraction to recover detector partition, hardware and module information.</li>
<li>Consolidate alarms into five classes: <strong>ALERT, EMR, NO CONNECTION, NO TOGGLE and TRIPPED</strong>.</li>
<li>Construct module-level alarm-count time series for forecasting and partition-level sequences for dominant alarm-type classification.</li>
<li>Preserve chronological order, scale from the training portion, generate sliding-window sequences and tune LSTM models.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Evaluation and evidence</h2>
<p>Forecasting and classification were evaluated chronologically rather than by randomly shuffling time-dependent observations. The LBA forecasting result is interpreted cautiously because overlapping sequences near the chronological split reduced independence between training and evaluation samples.</p>

<h2>Key results</h2>
<ul>
<li>LSTM alarm-count forecasting produced MAPE of approximately <strong>12.8–22.9%</strong> across the four selected forecasting cases.</li>
<li>The raw dominant-alarm classifier achieved <strong>88% accuracy</strong>, but the result was strongly affected by class imbalance.</li>
<li>After chattering-alarm reduction, accuracy was <strong>75%</strong> while macro-F1 improved from <strong>0.27 to 0.57</strong>.</li>
<li>TileCal alarm activity was strongly spatially non-uniform across partitions and modules.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Limitations and scope</h2>
<p>This is a <strong>proof-of-concept predictive-maintenance study</strong>, not a deployed ATLAS operational system. Limited sample sizes, class imbalance, validation design and the absence of live DCS testing constrain operational conclusions.</p>

<h2>Practical relevance</h2>
<p>The work shows how historical alarm streams could be converted into decision-support signals that help engineers focus attention on unusual equipment behaviour earlier. Any operational use would require further validation on live or prospectively collected detector data.</p>

<h2>What this case proves</h2>
<p>I can translate technically complex detector-control data into a machine-learning formulation, combine NLP extraction with time-series modelling, and interpret model results in the context of scientific operations rather than treating metrics in isolation.</p>

